/P 368 0 R
/E (HTTP \(Hypertext Transfer Protocol\) is the foundational, text-based protocol used to transfer data across the internet. It operates on a classic Client-Server model, where a client \(such as your laptop browser or smartphone\) sends an outbound request, and a server \(such as an ESP32 or a cloud database\) processes that request and sends back a response.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
575 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 368 0 R
/E (HTTP Protocol )
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
576 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 368 0 R
/T (Advantages of HTTP)
/E (Advantages of HTTP)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
577 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1090 0 R
/E (Universal Compatibility: It is the standard language of the web. Every browser, smartphone, cloud database, and modern programming language supports it natively out of the box.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
578 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1091 0 R
/E (Simple and Human-Readable: Because HTTP uses clear text headers and standard actions \(like GET and POST\), it is incredibly easy to read, write, and debug using simple tools.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
579 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1092 0 R
/E (Firewall Friendly: HTTP typically communicates on standard network ports \(Port 80 for unencrypted text and Port 443 for secure SSL\). Routers and corporate firewalls usually keep these ports open by default, preventing connection blocks.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
580 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1093 0 R
/E (Stateless Reliability: Each request is entirely independent and self-contained. If one transaction fails due to a temporary signal drop, it does not crash or corrupt the history of the next transmission.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
581 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 368 0 R
/T (Disadvantages of HTTP)
/E (Disadvantages of HTTP)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
582 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1094 0 R
/E (High Packet Overhead: HTTP is a heavy protocol. To send a single, tiny numeric character command \(like 1 or 0 to control an LED\), the chip must pack it inside hundreds of bytes of mandatory text headers, cookies, and metadata.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
583 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1095 0 R
/E (Inefficient Polling \(Latency\): To receive commands from the web, an ESP32 client must repeatedly open a connection, download a file, and tear down the connection over and over again. This continuous cycling drains processing power and causes a delay in action response.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
584 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1096 0 R
/E (High Power Consumption: Because opening and closing secure TCP/IP connection handshakes requires significant processor execution time and radio uptime, it drains battery power quickly compared to lightweight streaming alternatives.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
585 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1097 0 R
/E (One-Way Initiation: The communication must always be started by the client. An external web server cannot directly reach through a home router to push a sudden command down to an ESP32 client without a complex setup; it must wait for the chip to check in first.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
