589 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 370 0 R
/E (MQTT \(Message Queuing Telemetry Transport\) is a lightweight, publish-subscribe network protocol designed specifically for resource-constrained microcontrollers and low-bandwidth, high-latency networks.)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
590 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 370 0 R
/E (Instead of clients talking directly to a server, MQTT uses a central Broker. Devices connect to this broker and either Publish data to a specific text pathway \(called a Topic\) or Subscribe to a topic to listen for updates.)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
591 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 370 0 R
/E (MQTT Protocol )
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
592 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 370 0 R
/T (Advantages of MQTT)
/E (Advantages of MQTT)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
593 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1101 0 R
/E (Ultra-Lightweight and Fast: MQTT has a tiny packet overhead \(its fixed header is only 2 bytes\). This allows the ESP32 to transmit or receive commands with minimal network data, making operations feel instantaneous.)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
594 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1102 0 R
/E (Real-Time Event Pushing: Because it uses an asynchronous "push" model, you do not need to repeatedly query a file to check for updates. The broker actively pushes new data to the subscriber the millisecond it arrives.)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
595 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1103 0 R
/E (Low Power Consumption: Keeping a thin TCP/IP socket idle consumes drastically less battery power than continuously opening, downloading, and destroying heavy HTTP connection stacks.)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
596 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1104 0 R
/E (Bi-directional Through Firewalls: Once an ESP32 makes an initial outbound connection to a cloud broker, that pipeline stays open. This allows an external phone or computer to send commands down to the chip instantly, even if the hardware is buried deep behind a private home router firewall.)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
597 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 370 0 R
/T (Disadvantages of MQTT)
/E (Disadvantages of MQTT)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
598 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1105 0 R
/E (Requires a Central Broker: MQTT cannot function in a vacuum. You must deploy, maintain, or pay for a dedicated central broker server \(like Mosquitto, HiveMQ, or EMQX\) to handle the message traffic distribution.)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
599 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1106 0 R
/E (No Built-In Data History: Standard MQTT brokers are strictly data routers, not databases. They deliver a message to active subscribers and immediately delete it from memory. If you want to log data over time, you must attach a separate storage engine to the pipeline.)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
600 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1107 0 R
/E (Increased System Complexity: The asynchronous publish/subscribe model requires managing unique topic strings, subscription lists, and state variables, which is a steeper learning curve for beginners than basic web page fetching.)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
601 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1108 0 R
/E (Potential for Topic Crossovers: In a classroom or development setting, if multiple students accidentally use the exact same public broker and topic string, their devices will intercept each other's commands, causing random device actions.)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
